Revision history for Net-Firewall-BlockerHelper

0.1.0   2026-07-13
        - ipfw: emit family-correct rules. The ipfw 'me' keyword only matches
          IPv4 addresses (me6 exists for IPv6), so the previous 'to me' rules
          never blocked IPv6 traffic; family neutral protocols such as
          tcp/udp now get one rule per family (to me and to me6) and family
          specific ones a single rule for theirs. Reject rules pick
          unreach/unreach6 per rule family.
        - pf: no longer execute the kill pipelines when in testing mode; they
          are recorded like every other command.
        - re_init now treats teardown as best effort in all backends. With
          errors being fatal, a strict teardown of an externally wiped setup
          died and aborted the very re_init self-heal was attempting; init
          does its own cleanup of remnants.
        - Kill commands now handle both IPv4 and IPv6 in every backend that
          has them. iptables/firewalld/nftables/ufw conntrack passes -f ipv6
          for IPv6 IPs (conntrack defaults to IPv4 and would never kill IPv6
          flows). ipfw picks the sockstat family and uses IPv6-safe parsing,
          splitting only the port colon and stripping scope IDs, instead of
          being IPv4 only. pf kill-by-port matches the family-specific state
          format (IPv4 addr:port vs IPv6 addr[port]) instead of only ever
          matching IPv4 states.
        - nftables: gains the same conntrack based kill option as the
          iptables backend.
        - Kill commands cover UDP where the platform allows. conntrack -D -s
          (iptables/firewalld/nftables/ufw) and pf's state killing are not
          protocol filtered, so UDP was already covered and is now documented
          and pinned by tests. ufw's ss kill mode now passes -tu so connected
          UDP sockets are killed alongside TCP. ipfw remains TCP-only as
          FreeBSD has no tcpdrop equivalent for UDP; being stateless rules,
          UDP from a banned IP is blocked immediately regardless, which is
          now documented.
        - Kill commands are now scoped to the configured protocols, so an
          instance blocking only udp never kills tcp state and vice versa.
          conntrack kills (iptables/firewalld/nftables/ufw) are scoped via
          -p per blocked protocol, ufw's ss kill picks -t/-u/-tu (or nothing
          when neither is blocked), pf kills via the state table filtered to
          the blocked protocols and ports (pfctl -k, which kills everything,
          is only used when everything is being blocked), and ipfw skips
          tcpdrop when tcp is not among the blocked protocols. With no
          protocols configured everything is being blocked and the kills
          remain unscoped; ports without protocols scopes to tcp/udp.
        - Validate the combined prefix + name length against the kernel
          object name limits (iptables chains 28, ipset and pf tables 31,
          ipfw tables 63) at new, raising nameTooLong instead of failing
          with a confusing command error at init.
        - Add a nftables backend. A dedicated inet table is created per
          instance with a input-hooked chain, v4/v6 sets, and block rules,
          so teardown is a single table delete.
        - Add a firewalld backend using ipset plus firewall-cmd --direct
          rules, for hosts where raw iptables calls would fight firewalld.
        - Add a ufw backend using per-IP prepend/delete rules, with optional
          connection killing via ss or conntrack.
        - Add a route backend blocking whole IPs via iproute2 null routes
          (unreachable/blackhole/prohibit); works with no firewall at all.
        - Add a npf backend for NetBSD, managing IPs in a npf table declared
          in npf.conf.
        - Add a cloudflare backend managing IP access rules via the
          Cloudflare v4 API using LWP::UserAgent (equivalent to the fail2ban
          cloudflare/cloudflare-token actions, supporting both token and
          legacy email+key auth).
        - Add a netscaler backend binding IPs into a Citrix NetScaler/ADC
          policy dataset via the NITRO REST API using LWP::UserAgent.
        - Add a nsupdate backend maintaining a DNS based blocklist via BIND
          dynamic updates (IPv4 only, matching the fail2ban action).
        - LWP::UserAgent is loaded at run time only by the backends needing
          it and is listed as a recommends, not a requirement.
        - Security: anchor the IPv4/IPv6 validation regexps in the frontend
          and all backends, preventing shell command injection via crafted
          ban targets.
        - Fix runtime errors (failed ban/unban/init/teardown/flush commands
          and the like) being warnings instead of fatal as documented. The
          installed Error::Helper checks errorExtra all_fatal rather than the
          all_errors_fatal its POD documents, so command failures were
          silently swallowed; both keys are now set.
        - Deepen the check implementations so externally removed rules are
          noticed and self-heal actually fires: ipfw also verifies the rule
          number exists, pf also verifies the anchor still holds rules, and
          iptables verifies each block rule in the chain via iptables -C.
        - Fix the ipfw kill option: the UDP command was a no-op pipeline and
          has been removed, and the IP is now matched as a fixed word
          (grep -wF) so 1.2.3.4 no longer also matches the likes of
          11.2.3.45. The pf kill-by-port grep likewise now escapes the IP.
        - Fix the frontend reporting noBackendSpecified instead of
          invalidBackend (swapped error/perror fields) for a bad backend name.
        - Validate ports as 1-65535 instead of any positive int, so bad ports
          fail at new rather than as firewall command errors at init.
        - iptables: treat sctp as port-capable like ipfw/pf, and recognize
          the icmp6/icmpv6 aliases of ipv6-icmp when splitting protocols per
          family so they are never emitted into the v4 table.
        - Lowercase IPs on ban/unban so the same IPv6 IP in differing cases
          can not result in duplicate or orphaned entries.
        - Frontend methods now raise a clear error mentioning init_backend
          when called before init_backend instead of dying with a confusing
          undefined-value message.
        - Add tests: real (non-testing) command execution and failure error
          codes via the shell backend including end-to-end self-heal,
          teardown/re_init command content and ordering, duplicate-ban and
          unban-of-unbanned short-circuits, IPv6 routing and case
          normalization per backend, check probe depth, port boundaries, and
          exact error code/flag assertions.
        - Add a working iptables backend using ipset with iptables/ip6tables.
        - Add stop, check, and flush methods to the frontend and all backends,
          equivalent to fail2ban's actionstop, actioncheck, and actionflush.
        - Add optional self healing: verify the firewall setup before each
          ban/unban and re_init it if it was removed externally. On by default,
          controlled by the self_heal option (per-call overridable).
        - shell backend: add optional check and flush options.
        - Fix the pf backend so kill without ports kills all connections for
          the IP rather than doing nothing.
        - Fix the pf backend ban/unban using malformed pfctl table syntax
          (-T <table> add -> -t <table> -T add), which errored on real pf.
        - ipfw/pf: only attach ports to port-capable protocols (tcp/udp/sctp)
          and default to tcp/udp when ports are given without protocols,
          instead of generating port-on-ip/icmp rules that failed at init.
        - ipfw: block IPv4 and IPv6 by default (emit ip4 and ip6 rules; the
          ipfw 'ip' keyword means any packet, not IPv4, per ipfw(8)) and
          choose unreach/unreach6 per protocol family, so banned IPv6 addresses
          are actually blocked and reject rules are valid per family. The type
          option is now deny-vs-reject; unreach and unreach6 are the per-family
          reject codes (legacy type values still accepted).
        - Fix backends reporting the wrong error flag (invalidPrefixSpecified
          instead of invalidName) for a bad or missing name.
        - Fix exit-code checks to use numeric comparison instead of string.
        - Fix protocol validation error messages to reference getprotobyname
          instead of getservbyname.
        - Modernize init_backend to load the backend with require instead of a
          constructed string eval.
        - Only write test_data when running in testing mode.
        - Documentation and packaging fixes: correct SYNOPSIS backend names,
          update the README backend list, fix a malformed POD directive, add
          MANIFEST entries, and add tests for the new functionality.

0.0.1   2025-07-09/22:00
        - Initial release.

